drm/ttm: don't reclaim in the caller for the beneficial order

Since a driver can name the order it benefits from, the pool tries that
order with __GFP_RETRY_MAYFAIL in place of __GFP_NORETRY. For a costly
order, that has the allocating thread reclaim and compact over and over
while memory is fragmented, until neither makes progress, before it
falls back to the lower orders it would have taken anyway. Every other
order above zero already skips reclaim.

On a Panther Lake laptop with xe, whose beneficial order is 2 MiB, a
compositor's main thread was seen stalled on memory for 0.1 to 1.3 s at
a time, each stall beside failing direct compaction and direct reclaim
and no huge-page fault.

Clear __GFP_DIRECT_RECLAIM at the beneficial order and keep
__GFP_KSWAPD_RECLAIM, so kswapd and kcompactd make 2 MiB blocks for the
next allocation while this one falls back, as THP's defer does.

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/ttm/ttm_pool.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/ttm/ttm_pool.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/ttm/ttm_pool.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/ttm/ttm_pool.c
@@ -183,10 +183,15 @@
 	if (order && beneficial_order && order != beneficial_order)
 		gfp_flags &= ~__GFP_RECLAIM;
 
-	if (beneficial_order && order == beneficial_order) {
-		gfp_flags &= ~__GFP_NORETRY;
-		gfp_flags |= __GFP_RETRY_MAYFAIL;
-	}
+	/*
+	 * Nor at the order the device does gain from: reclaiming and
+	 * compacting for it in the caller held a compositor's thread for over
+	 * a second while memory was fragmented. Wake kswapd, which wakes
+	 * kcompactd, to make such blocks for the next allocation, and let
+	 * this one fall back to the lower orders, as THP's defer does.
+	 */
+	if (beneficial_order && order == beneficial_order)
+		gfp_flags &= ~__GFP_DIRECT_RECLAIM;
 
 	if (!ttm_pool_uses_dma_alloc(pool)) {
 		p = alloc_pages_node(pool->nid, gfp_flags, order);
